{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgiiYz2uwkwXp7FjySYy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate is written and passes the self-check, which printed `OK`. On the short smoke fit (100 draws, 1 chain) it scored ELPD-LOO −3279.6, about 6 nats better than the model it refines (−3285.2). That number isn't reliable yet: 14.7% of trials had a high Pareto k, so the PSIS-LOO estimate is flagged as untrustworthy. I'd expect that to settle with the full 4-chain fit, but the check doesn't show it.\n\n**What I chose:** I refined `streak_excess_pattern_rule_built`, the closest non-incumbent model (rank 1, 19 nats behind the best). I didn't refine the incumbent because it already has its own refinement slots this round.\n\n**The one change:** the streak penalty now applies to the run the sequence *ends* on, instead of the longest run anywhere. Each repeated flip in the final run (…HH, …TTT) counts against a sequence looking random, and a sequence that ends on a switch gets no penalty. The penalty is shared by everyone, and the data decide its sign. Everything else in the model is unchanged: the personal expected tally span, switching judged against an ideal rate, chunk variety and the rule-built penalty scaled by a personal pattern sensitivity, and the left/right lean.\n\n**Why:** it targets the critique's strongest discrepancy. Among pairs with equal switch counts, people pick the sequence that ends on a switch more often than the best model predicts (observed 0.521 vs 0.458 predicted, p = 0.01). That result doesn't survive the false-discovery correction (q = 0.08), so it's the leading lead rather than an established effect.\n\n**Not checked:**\n- **Novelty:** the check doesn't compare against the live models.\n- **Convergence:** with one chain, R-hat can't be computed.\n- **Speed:** each smoke run took 5–6 minutes. The full fit must finish within the 30-minute admission limit, and I haven't confirmed